From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Vysotsky <il.mohoff2016@yandex.ru>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 08:16:26 +0400
Subject: [PATCH 06/30] swgl GPU path: loops that fit, clip distances, GPU
 copies, feedback reads

- Loops are unrolled to 32, 16 or 8 iterations, whichever is the largest
  that fits ps_2_x's 512 instructions. This brings the blurs (at most six
  steps once WebRender scales the deviation down to 4) and the gradient
  stop tree (five levels for 625 stops) onto the GPU.
- Vertex shaders that write gl_ClipDistance pass the distances as one more
  interpolated varying, and the pixel shader discards where any is
  negative, which is the clip SWGL applies. Glyph-transformed text now
  runs on the GPU too.
- BlitFramebuffer and CopyImageSubData copy on the GPU with
  CopySubresourceRegion when the source's GPU copy is current, so backdrop
  copies and texture cache moves need no readback. The CPU blit now marks
  its destination as written.
- A draw that samples its own render target reads from a copy, because
  Direct3D unbinds one binding when the other is set.

Only cs_svg_filter_node and the dual-source glyph-transform text variant
(no dual-source blending at 9_3) stay on the CPU. On the PC, 133 of the
144 filter reftests match the CPU render. Most remaining differences are a
few hundred antialiased edge pixels on rotated content. One real bug is
still open: backdrop-filter-isolation.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
